-# Phase 3B independent code review
-
-## Result
-
-- codeQualityStatus: BLOCK
-- recommendation: REQUEST_CHANGES
-
-## Skill-perspective check
-
-`remove-ai-slops` and `programming` were not available in the supplied skill catalog or local skill roots. I applied their stated review criteria directly. The diff violates both perspectives: the tests exercise isolated helper functions rather than the production worker path, giving false confidence; the regex command parser is brittle, implementation-shaped validation that cannot enforce the requested boundary.
-
-## CRITICAL
-
-- `daemon/src/tool-home.ts:18-20` launches the actual vendor Codex process directly with `spawn`; no production caller invokes `runEnforcedWorker` (`daemon/src/worker-enforcement.ts:38-50`). Therefore the alleged filesystem and network enforcement is not in the real worker execution path. P3-9 and P3-16 can be bypassed by every actual Codex worker execution. The P3B tests call the helper directly (`daemon/test/p3b.test.ts:42,51,63,71,73`), so their PASS result does not establish Cue enforcement.
-
-- `daemon/src/worker-enforcement.ts:30-40` tries to block filesystem writes by regex-scraping absolute paths from argv. This neither observes nor intercepts filesystem operations. A worker can derive an absolute target from environment/stdin/a file or use a relative traversal/shell expansion, and the process is then executed. It is not a realpath boundary monitor and cannot meet the accepted P1-2 requirement beyond the test’s literal command string.
-
-- `daemon/src/worker-enforcement.ts:8-24,38-48` has a check-then-use race: it resolves the candidate before spawning, without holding an OS-level handle or enforcing subsequent opens. A path component can be replaced with a junction/symlink after the check and before the child writes, defeating the realpath result.
-
-- `daemon/src/worker-enforcement.ts:43-49` supplies `NODE_OPTIONS` to the launched executable; `daemon/src/network-guard.cjs:1-32` only monkey-patches Node JavaScript APIs. It does not apply an OS/process-level egress boundary to arbitrary worker descendants (for example PowerShell/curl/native code), and the real vendor launch does not receive it at all. `daemon/test/p3b.test.ts:61-64` only proves a Node `fetch` call fails, not the required worker-process external egress block.
-
-## HIGH
-
-- Enforcement violations are never recorded automatically: `recordEnforcementViolation` has no caller outside the tests (`daemon/src/worker-enforcement.ts:53-56`; tests at `daemon/test/p3b.test.ts:55,77`). Likewise `completeTaskIfEnforced` has no production caller. Completion is thus not actually blocked after an escape/gate failure.
-
-- Execution accounting is disconnected: `recordExecution` has no production caller outside `p3b.test.ts` (`daemon/src/execution-accounting.ts:5-15`). The required approval-versus-execution ledger and alert will not be produced for live operations. Further, it emits a new alert after every excess execution rather than one identifiable alert for the imbalance.
-
-- Required artifacts are incomplete at review time: `evidence/P3/p3b_result.json` and `evidence/P3/P3B_VERDICT.md` do not exist. `p3b_test_output.log` records a passing unit suite, but is not evidence of the mandated actual Cue worker boundaries.
-
-## MEDIUM
-
-- `daemon/test/p3b.test.ts:34-35` checks `startsWith(` only in two selected files. It neither audits all path-comparison code paths nor distinguishes a benign method/header `startsWith` from a path boundary operation. This is a brittle source-string test rather than behavior evidence.
-
-- `daemon/test/p3b.test.ts:42` supplies `inspectedPaths:[target]`, pre-declaring the symlink escape to the helper. That does not prove a worker command whose target appears only at runtime is caught. The same implementation-shaped pattern appears in the absolute write test’s literal path at lines 48-52.
-
-- `daemon/src/deadlock.ts:1-13` is an isolated in-memory model with no integration to declined approvals or task state. The tests only verify its local return values (`daemon/test/p3b.test.ts:99-104`), not that a real outside-envelope request triggers the deadlock behavior.
-
-- `daemon/test/p3b.test.ts:96` describes an “outside-envelope request” but only disables `allowed_actions` for an otherwise in-worktree command. It does not exercise an outside worktree path.
-
-## LOW
-
-- `daemon/test/p3b.test.ts:2` imports `writeFileSync` unused. Formatting is highly compressed, which obscures security-critical control flow.
-
-## Evidence checked
-
-I independently ran `npm test` in `daemon`: build passed and Vitest reported 3 files / 58 tests passed. The existing `evidence/P3/p3b_test_output.log` matches that type of output. This only substantiates unit-helper behavior; it cannot substantiate the success claims above because the live vendor worker path is unprotected.
-
-## Blockers
-
-1. Route the actual vendor worker and all command descendants through a genuine enforceable filesystem boundary; do not rely on argv parsing.
-2. Apply a real process/OS-level egress gate to the actual worker and its descendants, then demonstrate `example.com` failure and allowlist behavior with that worker.
-3. Wire violation recording, completion prevention, execution recording, and deadlock state to live event/worker paths.
-4. Add the two required result/verdict artifacts only after their claims match live evidence.
